WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.122
The reasoning patterns in this module transfer well across database systems,

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:08.857
even when the exact syntax changes.

00:00:08.857 --> 00:00:12.802
AND, OR, NOT, IS NULL, COALESCE, NULLIF, and CASE are widely supported.

00:00:12.802 --> 00:00:17.392
But some details are more platform-specific.

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:22.557
IS DISTINCT FROM is available in PostgreSQL and several modern systems,

00:00:22.557 --> 00:00:25.907
but not every platform handles NULL-safe comparison with the same syntax.

00:00:25.907 --> 00:00:30.912
Date arithmetic is another common difference.

00:00:30.912 --> 00:00:34.657
PostgreSQL can subtract dates directly in many cases.

00:00:34.657 --> 00:00:39.962
SQL Server and Microsoft Fabric often use functions such as DATEDIFF.

00:00:39.962 --> 00:00:46.107
Other databases may use interval functions or date-difference functions with different argument order.

00:00:46.107 --> 00:00:53.097
The best way to move between SQL dialects is to preserve the rule before translating the syntax.

00:00:53.097 --> 00:01:00.277
For example: “I need the number of days between admission and discharge.” That is the logic.

00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:03.642
The PostgreSQL expression is one implementation.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:09.402
A different platform may use another function, but the analytical meaning stays the same.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:13.992
The same is true for NULL handling and CASE rules.

00:01:13.992 --> 00:01:17.732
First define the intended cohort and classifications.

00:01:17.732 --> 00:01:21.012
Then translate database-specific functions.

00:01:21.012 --> 00:01:27.442
If you learn SQL only as memorized syntax, moving to another platform feels difficult.

00:01:27.442 --> 00:01:31.798
If you learn the reasoning, the new syntax becomes a smaller problem.
